End-to-end works with cache. Now try the dual-stream interleave experiment (v5) on top of v4, and PGO.

v5 design: each thread gets 2 chunks; two cursors; process alternately one line from each; separate tables tA/tB (avoid store-forward conflicts on same entries); at end merge both into job table... Actually simpler: keep ONE table but alternate lines — the dependency chain per line: probe load → compare → update stores; alternating streams lets line N+2's probe proceed while line N finishes. Table shared is fine for ILP? The stores are to same cache lines potentially — different stations interleave randomly, mostly independent. Sharing keeps L1 footprint smaller. But risk of store-to-load forwarding stalls minimal. I'll use a single table, two cursors.

Loop structure:
```
const char *pa=startA,*ea=endA,*pb=startB,*eb=endB;
while (pa<ea || pb<eb) {
  if (pa<ea) { process line at pa; advance pa; }
  if (pb<eb) { ... }
}
```
Branches pa<ea twice per line — predicted fine (long runs).

Splitting: chunk boundaries as now but 2*nthreads chunks; thread i takes chunks 2i and 2i+1.

Actually even better: 4 cursors per thread? Diminishing; try 2 first.

Implement v5 quickly via python patch on v4: modify Job struct to have start2/end2, worker loop.

